---
title: "Event Delivery & Retries"
description: "How Outpost delivers events, tracks attempts, and retries on failure."
---

Outpost tracks each event, its status, and all delivery attempts. Delivery operates on an **at-least-once guarantee** — events may occasionally be re-delivered. Consumers should deduplicate using the **event id** (the same value as publish `id` when you set one). For how that appears on webhook requests — headers, prefix, Standard Webhooks — see [Event ID header and idempotency](/docs/outpost/destinations/webhook#event-id-header-and-idempotency).

## Automatic Retries

When a delivery attempt fails — because the destination is unavailable, misconfigured, or returns a non-2xx HTTP status — Outpost automatically retries the event.

By default, retries use an **exponential backoff** algorithm with a base of `2`, or you can define a custom retry schedule.

{% tabs tabGroup="deployment" %}
{% tab label="Managed" %}
You can configure retry behavior in the [Hookdeck General settings](https://dashboard.hookdeck.com/settings/project/general) or with the Config API using the `MAX_RETRY_LIMIT`, `RETRY_INTERVAL_SECONDS`, and `RETRY_SCHEDULE` variables.

| Variable | Default | Description |
|----------|---------|-------------|
| `MAX_RETRY_LIMIT` | `10` | Maximum number of retry attempts before giving up |
| `RETRY_INTERVAL_SECONDS` | `30` | Base interval in seconds for exponential backoff |
| `RETRY_SCHEDULE` | — | Comma-separated retry delays in seconds. Overrides `MAX_RETRY_LIMIT` and `RETRY_INTERVAL_SECONDS` when set |

Example custom retry schedule:

```json
{
  "RETRY_SCHEDULE": [30, 60, 300, 900, 3600]
}
```
{% /tab %}
{% tab label="Self-Hosted" %}
Configure retry behavior with environment variables:

| Variable | Default | Description |
|----------|---------|-------------|
| `MAX_RETRY_LIMIT` | `10` | Maximum number of retry attempts before giving up |
| `RETRY_INTERVAL_SECONDS` | `30` | Base interval in seconds for exponential backoff |
| `RETRY_SCHEDULE` | — | Comma-separated retry delays in seconds. Overrides `MAX_RETRY_LIMIT` and `RETRY_INTERVAL_SECONDS` when set |

Example custom retry schedule:

```
RETRY_SCHEDULE=30,60,300,900,3600
```
{% /tab %}
{% /tabs %}

## Retry Timing

Retry delays are approximate. Outpost stores delivery attempts in batches, and a retry reads the attempt that triggered it, so a retry does not run before that attempt is stored. A delay shorter than `LOG_BATCH_THRESHOLD_SECONDS` (default `10`) is raised to it. Outpost logs a warning at startup listing the configured delays that are raised.

The effective delay of a retry is about:

- the larger of the configured delay and `LOG_BATCH_THRESHOLD_SECONDS`
- plus up to `RETRY_POLL_BACKOFF_MS` when you set it explicitly (the default, `0`, wakes the retry monitor when the next retry is due)
- plus up to `RETRY_LOOKUP_BATCH_WAIT_MS` when `RETRY_LOOKUP_BATCH_SIZE` is above `1`

If the attempt is still not stored when the retry runs, the retry waits about `LOG_BATCH_THRESHOLD_SECONDS` again (varied by up to 25%) and looks again.

For example, with `RETRY_SCHEDULE=1,5,10`, the defaults and `RETRY_LOOKUP_BATCH_SIZE=50`, each of the three retries runs about 10 seconds after its failed attempt, plus up to 50 ms for batching. With `RETRY_SCHEDULE=1,5,30` the third retry keeps its 30 seconds.

For tighter timing, set `RETRY_LOOKUP_BATCH_SIZE=0` and lower `LOG_BATCH_THRESHOLD_SECONDS`. A lower threshold flushes logs more often, which means more log store writes. Batching lookups helps high-volume deployments; at low volume, or on PostgreSQL, leave it disabled.

## Manual Retries

Any failed delivery attempt can be manually retried via the [Retry API](/docs/outpost/api#retry-event-delivery), the tenant portal or Admin UI. Manual retries are available for attempts that have exhausted automatic retries or were skipped due to the destination being disabled.

## Disabled Destinations

If a destination is disabled — through the API, tenant portal, or automatically due to a [failure threshold](/docs/outpost/features/operator-events) — events published to that tenant will not be delivered to it. Disabled destinations cannot be retried until re-enabled.

## Delivery Attempts

Each delivery attempt records:
- The destination it was sent to
- The HTTP status code and response body (for webhook destinations)
- The timestamp and attempt number
- Whether automatic retries are exhausted

Access delivery attempts via the [API Reference](/docs/outpost/api#attempts), the tenant portal, or Admin UI.

Attempts of a deleted destination stay in the event log: list them with the `destination_id` filter on [list attempts](/docs/outpost/api/attempts#list-attempts). The deleted destination's own endpoints, including its attempts endpoints, return `404`.

To receive attempts as they happen, subscribe to the `attempt.success` and `attempt.failed` [operator events](/docs/outpost/features/operator-events). They fire once per delivery attempt.
